c=concept(934,'Digital-camera pipeline','Optics, silicon photosites, analog gain, digitization and the explicitly drawn ISP sequence.')
ask(c,'Which order matches the camera front end before ISP processing in the figure?', ['Optics → CCD/CMOS sensor → ISO gain → ADC','Optics → ADC → CCD/CMOS sensor → ISO gain','ISO gain → optics → ADC → CCD/CMOS sensor','CCD/CMOS sensor → ISP → optics → ADC'],['The arrows lead from optical collection to sensing, amplification and digitization.','An ADC digitizes the sensor signal rather than preceding its creation.','The drawn gain acts on the sensed signal, not before the optics.','The optical stage precedes sensing and ISP in the figure.'],kind='diagram',locator='Main pipeline from Real World to ISP',evidence='Real World → Optics (Lens/Aperture/shutter) → Sensor CCD/CMOS → Gain ISO → ADC → ISP.')
ask(c,'Following the arrows inside the simplified ISP block, which sequence is actually shown?', ['Demosaic → Denoise → White balance → Gamma → Compress','Denoise → Demosaic → Gamma → White balance → Compress','White balance → Compress → Demosaic → Gamma → Denoise','Gamma → Demosaic → Denoise → Compress → White balance'],['This follows the down, right, up and right arrows in the printed block.','This exchanges the first two and middle processing roles relative to the drawing.','The figure does not compress before demosaicing.','The figure places gamma near the output, after white balance.'],kind='diagram',locator='ISP block arrows',evidence='ISP arrows connect Demosaic ↓ Denoise → White balance ↑ Gamma → Compress → RGB Image.',assumptions=['Question asks about the displayed simplified pipeline, not a mandatory ordering in every commercial camera.'])
ask(c,'Which statement best describes the photosites discussed with the pipeline?', ['A regular array of light-sensitive silicon sites converts incident light into electrical signals','A regular array of mechanical shutters directly stores semantic object labels','A grid of lens elements replaces all electronic signal conversion','A set of software pixels exists before any light reaches the sensor'],['The sensor array performs the light-to-electrical acquisition step.','The sites are not described as shutters or semantic memories.','Optics and electronic sensing have distinct roles.','The described array is physical sensing hardware, not merely a later software image.'],locator='Photosite and retina analogy')
c=concept(935,'CCD and CMOS readout','Charge transfer versus local conversion, acronym qualification and shared sensing function.')
ask(c,'Which readout contrast matches the two sensor diagrams?', ['CCD transfers charge toward common conversion/output circuitry; CMOS permits local charge-to-voltage conversion and addressed readout','CCD converts every pixel through an optical lens; CMOS stores photons without electrical conversion','CCD performs software color correction before capture; CMOS performs no amplification','CCD reads only red pixels; CMOS reads only green pixels'],['The figure contrasts shifted charge readout with pixel-associated electronics.','Both technologies perform electronic photo-sensing, not the described alternatives.','Both require acquisition/readout; ISP color correction is a later function.','Color filtering is not the defining CCD/CMOS distinction.'],kind='diagram',locator='CCD and CMOS conversion/readout schematic')
ask(c,'Which task is shared by the two image-sensor technologies despite their different readout architectures?', ['Turning incident light into an electrical image signal','Determining the unique 3D scene from a single image without processing','Encoding every pixel directly into a JPEG bitstream inside the photodiode','Selecting the robot’s collision-free path without algorithms'],['Both are photosensor technologies used at the capture stage.','Image formation is not unique 3D reconstruction.','JPEG is downstream processing rather than the defining photodiode function.','Navigation requires interpretation/planning beyond the sensor.'],pages=[934,935],locator='Sensor-stage description and CCD/CMOS comparison')
ask(c,'The source expands CMOS as “Complementary Metal Oxide on Silicon.” How should this wording be handled when explaining the readout comparison?', ['Flag the expansion as a source issue while retaining the separate local-conversion/readout concept','Treat the unusual expansion as proof that CMOS has no semiconductor circuitry','Infer that CCD has no silicon because the printed CMOS phrase mentions silicon','Discard the entire CCD/CMOS readout comparison because one expansion is inaccurate'],['The acronym wording and the readout distinction are separable; the standard expansion is complementary metal-oxide-semiconductor.','CMOS is semiconductor technology; the source’s phrasing does not prove otherwise.','The acronym phrase does not establish the material composition of CCDs.','A localized wording issue does not invalidate every independent diagram claim.'],conflict=(935,'CMOS is printed as Complementary Metal Oxide on Silicon.','Hamamatsu’s technical note uses complementary metal-oxide semiconductor; explicitly flag the expansion rather than silently accepting it. The readout contrast is assessed independently. https://hub.hamamatsu.com/us/en/technical-notes/image-sensors/advances-in-cmos-image-sensors.html'),locator='CMOS expansion and readout paragraph')
issue(935,'cmos-expansion','CMOS expansion printed Complementary Metal Oxide on Silicon.','Explicitly distinguish standard complementary metal-oxide-semiconductor, supported by Hamamatsu technical note https://hub.hamamatsu.com/us/en/technical-notes/image-sensors/advances-in-cmos-image-sensors.html ; preserve the valid readout comparison.')
c=concept(936,'Color sampling, gain and quantization','Bayer pattern proportions, missing-channel reconstruction, ISO amplification and ADC.')
ask(c,'Which counts describe one 2×2 tile of the illustrated Bayer color filter array?', ['Two green, one red, one blue','One green, two red, one blue','One green, one red, two blue','Four sites each measuring all three colors independently'],['The shown tile allocates half its sites to green and one quarter to each other color.','This exchanges the red and green proportions.','This exchanges the blue and green proportions.','A CFA site measures a filtered channel rather than all three independent channels.'],kind='diagram',locator='Bayer color filter grid')
ask(c,'For four Bayer photosites, each supplying one scalar channel value, how many channel values must be estimated to form four RGB triplets?', ['8','4','12','2'],['Four RGB triplets require12 channel entries;4 are sampled and8 are missing.','Four counts the measured entries, not the missing ones.','Twelve is the total output-entry count.','Two is the number missing at each site, not across all four sites.'],kind='numerical',pages=[936,937],steps=['Four output pixels require4×3=12 channel values.','The four CFA sites supply4 measured values.','Missing values=12−4=8.'],formula=[fm('N_missing=3N−N=2N',{'N':'number of CFA photosites/output RGB pixels','N_missing':'channel entries to estimate'},{'N,N_missing':'counts'},['One measured channel per site; one RGB triplet per output site; no extra measurements.'])],locator='Bayer sampling and two-missing-color demosaicing description')
ask(c,'Increasing the analog ISO gain at fixed illumination and exposure time most directly does what in the shown model?', ['Amplifies the sensed electrical signal before ADC without creating extra incident photons','Collects more photons solely because the numeric gain setting increased','Changes the physical Bayer pattern from green to red','Moves the camera’s principal point to the sensor edge'],['Gain is placed after the sensor; it scales a signal already generated by exposure.','Photon collection is not increased by downstream electrical multiplication alone.','Electronic gain does not rearrange the filters.','Gain is not a geometric calibration parameter.'],kind='scenario',locator='Gain amplification and ADC paragraphs',conflict=(936,'Gain is described as ensuring good exposure without distinguishing signal amplification from light collection.','At fixed illumination/exposure, electronic gain amplifies the electrical signal; it does not increase incident photons. Scope the claim to the shown gain stage.'))
ask(c,'A supplied ideal 3-bit ADC uses code q=floor(8V/Vmax), clipped to 0…7. At V=0.6Vmax, what code is produced?', ['4','5','3','8'],['floor(8×0.6)=floor(4.8)=4.','Five uses nearest-integer rounding rather than the supplied floor rule.','Three would correspond to a lower input bin.','Eight is outside a3-bit code range and also ignores the input fraction.'],kind='numerical',steps=['Normalize the input:V/Vmax=0.6.','Compute8×0.6=4.8 and apply floor to obtain4.','No clipping is needed because4 lies in0…7.'],formula=[fm('q=min(7,max(0,floor(8V/Vmax)))',{'q':'ADC output code','V':'input voltage','Vmax':'positive full-scale voltage'},{'q':'dimensionless integer','V,Vmax':'V'},['Illustrative ideal uniform3-bit ADC; floor-and-clip rule supplied by the question, not specified by the slide.'])],assumptions=['Use the explicitly supplied quantizer rather than assuming a rounding convention from the lecture.'],locator='ADC converts continuous voltage to a discrete number')
issue(936,'gain-exposure','Gain/exposure prose can conflate amplification with additional light collection.','State fixed illumination and exposure when distinguishing electrical gain from photon count.')
c=concept(937,'ISP reconstruction and noise','Demosaicing, photon shot noise, dark current, denoising and sharpening limits.')
ask(c,'What is the main reconstruction performed by demosaicing in this lecture?', ['Estimating the two missing color channels at each CFA site from neighboring measurements','Moving the lens until the image plane reaches focus','Converting every analog voltage directly to a binary bit','Estimating the camera’s external position from a world map'],['A CFA records one filtered value per site; demosaicing reconstructs the missing components.','Lens focusing is an optical operation.','Voltage quantization is the ADC’s role.','Pose estimation is geometric localization, not demosaicing.'],locator='Demosaicing paragraph')
ask(c,'Which noise-origin match follows the source?', ['Shot noise: variability in photon counts; dark current: thermally generated electrons','Shot noise: lens-focus error; dark current: wrong principal point','Shot noise: RGB channel interpolation; dark current: image compression','Shot noise: camera translation; dark current: object rotation'],['These are the two stated sensor-noise mechanisms.','Focus and principal-point errors are not the named stochastic charge sources.','Interpolation/compression may introduce artifacts but are not these mechanisms.','Geometric motion is not the described photon/thermal noise.'],kind='match',locator='Shot noise and dark-current bullets')
ask(c,'Which processing choice directly targets visible random graininess in a low-light image?', ['Denoising','Changing the world-to-camera pose matrix','Relabeling the principal point','Increasing edge sharpening alone'],['The source identifies denoising as noise/grain suppression.','A geometric transform does not by itself suppress sensor noise.','Relabeling coordinates leaves noise values unchanged.','Sharpening can accentuate high-frequency noise rather than specifically remove it.'],kind='scenario',locator='Denoising paragraph')
ask(c,'Which limitation should accompany the slide’s description of sharpening?', ['Edge enhancement can compensate for apparent blur but does not guarantee recovery of information lost during capture','Sharpening is identical to collecting more photons during exposure','Sharpening always eliminates both shot noise and dark current','Sharpening determines the true object depth from any blurred image'],['Enhancing existing edge contrast is not a guarantee of recovering unrecorded detail.','Post-capture processing cannot retroactively change photon collection.','Noise suppression and sharpening have different objectives.','Depth recovery is not the stated sharpening function.'],locator='Sharpening paragraph')
c=concept(938,'White balance, color correction and encoding','Channel gains, color-space maps, overloaded matrix C, gamma and compression.')
ask(c,'A neutral patch gives raw normalized RGB=(0.5,0.4,0.25). Which gains map it to(1,1,1) by channelwise multiplication?', ['(2,2.5,4)','(0.5,0.4,0.25)','(4,2.5,2)','(2,2,2)'],['Each gain is the reciprocal of its channel value.','These values would square rather than equalize the channel intensities.','The red and blue gains are exchanged.','A common gain preserves the original channel imbalance.'],kind='numerical',steps=['Red gain=1/0.5=2; green gain=1/0.4=2.5; blue gain=1/0.25=4.','Multiplying gives(1,1,1).'],formula=[fm('y_i=g_i x_i',{'i':'R,G or B channel','x_i':'raw normalized channel value','g_i':'white-balance gain','y_i':'corrected channel value'},{'x_i,g_i,y_i,i':'dimensionless'},['Positive patch values; target(1,1,1); no clipping, noise or nonlinear processing.'])],locator='White-balance gain description')
ask(c,'Why does the lecture introduce a color-correction matrix after discussing white balance?', ['Camera filter responses do not exactly match desired human/color-space responses, so channels may need mixing as well as gains','White balance already determines the camera’s 3D position','A matrix is needed because a Bayer array records full RGB at every site without interpolation','Color correction converts a scene point’s depth into a pixel row'],['The matrix maps sensor color responses toward a chosen standard color representation.','White balance addresses channel color balance, not pose.','The CFA does not measure full RGB at every site.','This describes geometric projection, not color correction.'],locator='Color correction toward CIE XYZ/sRGB paragraph')
ask(c,'The slide permits a3×4 color-correction matrix C, while later slides use a3×4 geometric camera matrix C. What distinguishes them?', ['Their input/output meanings: augmented color values versus homogeneous spatial coordinates','Their dimensions alone, because a3×4 matrix cannot be used for both','The geometric C must act on red, green and blue values only','The color C must contain the camera’s world translation'],['The same dimensions and symbol do not establish the same physical map.','Different linear maps can share dimensions.','Geometric projection uses spatial coordinates.','Color correction need not encode world pose.'],pages=[938,943,946],locator='Color matrix paragraph versus geometric camera-matrix equations',assumptions=['A3×4 color example would require an explicitly defined augmented color input; the source does not supply its fourth coordinate.'])
ask(c,'Using the supplied encoding y=x^(1/2) for normalized intensity x∈[0,1], what is y at x=0.25, and which operation is this illustrating?', ['0.5; nonlinear gamma-like encoding','0.0625; the stated square-root encoding','0.25; JPEG compression necessarily leaves every sample unchanged','2; white-balance gain applied independently of input'],['sqrt(0.25)=0.5; a power-law map illustrates the discussed gamma stage.','0.0625 squares the input instead of taking its square root.','The supplied nonlinear rule changes this value and is not a JPEG specification.','Two is the reciprocal square root, not the encoded value.'],kind='numerical',steps=['The supplied exponent is1/2, so take the square root.','sqrt(0.25)=0.5.'],formula=[fm('y=x^(1/2)',{'x':'normalized linear input','y':'encoded output'},{'x,y':'dimensionless'},['Illustrative exponent supplied for this question;0≤x≤1; not an exact sRGB transfer function or a source-specified gamma constant.'])],locator='Gamma power-law encoding paragraph')
ask(c,'Which pair correctly separates the final encoding/compression roles?', ['Gamma changes the intensity representation; compression reduces the stored/transmitted representation','Gamma estimates missing Bayer channels; compression changes lens focal length','Gamma estimates camera pose; compression measures thermal electrons','Gamma makes all RGB values equal; compression reconstructs metric depth'],['The stages have distinct signal-encoding and data-reduction purposes.','Demosaicing and lens geometry are different stages.','Neither stage is pose estimation or thermal sensing.','Equalizing a neutral patch belongs to white balance; depth recovery is another problem.'],pages=[934,938],locator='Gamma prose and final Compress block')
persist()
c=concept(939,'Image-formation geometry','Surface reflection, collecting optics, rear physical sensor and front virtual image plane.')
ask(c,'Which distinction is visible in the geometric image-formation diagram?', ['The physical sensor is behind the optics, while a virtual image plane is drawn in front','The physical sensor and virtual plane must occupy the same location','The surface normal is the camera’s principal point','Every reflected ray is collected by the sensor regardless of direction'],['The drawing separates the rear sensor from the front virtual construction.','They serve different geometric roles in the depicted model.','A surface normal describes surface orientation, not an image coordinate.','Only light entering the camera optics contributes through the illustrated optical path.'],kind='diagram',locator='Light source, surface normal, optics O, virtual image plane and sensor plane')
ask(c,'What is the role of the arrow marked with the surface normal in this figure?', ['It indicates local surface orientation, distinct from the reflected ray traveling toward the camera','It gives the focal length in pixels','It gives a unique depth for every sensor pixel','It indicates the direction of increasing image row index'],['A normal is perpendicular to the local surface; the reflected viewing ray is separately drawn.','Focal length describes the camera, not that surface arrow.','One surface normal does not determine all scene depths.','The local surface orientation is not the sensor’s row axis.'],kind='diagram',locator='Surface patch and normal arrow')
c=concept(940,'Pinhole image formation','Ray crossing produces an inverted real image, with small-aperture light loss and ideal-model limits.')
ask(c,'Why is the real pinhole image inverted in the drawing?', ['Rays from the object’s top cross the aperture and reach the lower part of the rear image plane','The ADC changes the sign of the measured brightness','The color filter array exchanges red and blue sites','The object must physically rotate during exposure'],['Straight rays through the small opening reverse top/bottom on the rear plane.','Digitization is not the cause of geometric inversion.','Color-filter order does not reverse object geometry.','A stationary object can produce the inverted projection.'],kind='diagram',locator='Object arrow, pinhole and inverted image arrow')
ask(c,'Which trade-off motivates replacing a tiny pinhole with a lens in the following slide?', ['A tiny aperture admits little light; a lens can collect light over a larger area','A pinhole requires RGB demosaicing but a lens eliminates all color sampling','A pinhole needs a world map but a lens automatically provides one','A lens guarantees perfect focus at all depths without any optical assumptions'],['Light collection is the stated advantage of the lens model.','Demosaicing depends on sensor sampling, not whether a lens replaces a pinhole.','Neither acquisition model automatically produces a world map.','A real lens has focusing/depth limitations; the source does not guarantee universal perfection.'],pages=[940,941],locator='Pinhole dim-image and lens light-collection descriptions')
c=concept(941,'Thin-lens relation','Real-distance lens equation, unit consistency, focal limit, magnification and sign-convention scope.')
LENS=fm('1/d_o+1/d_i=1/f; d_i=f d_o/(d_o−f); |m|=d_i/d_o',{'d_o':'positive real-object distance','d_i':'positive real-image distance','f':'positive converging-lens focal length','m':'signed transverse magnification; negative for the illustrated inverted real image'},{'d_o,d_i,f':'same length unit, stated as mm in examples','m':'dimensionless'},['Ideal thin converging lens; real object beyond the focal point d_o>f; real inverted image; no lens distortion or thickness corrections.'])
ask(c,'For the illustrated real-object/real-image configuration, a thin lens has f=50 mm and object distance d_o=200 mm. What image distance follows?', ['200/3 mm, about66.7 mm','40 mm','150 mm','250 mm'],['d_i=50×200/(200−50)=200/3 mm.','40 results from incorrectly using200+50 in the denominator.','150 subtracts focal length directly instead of solving the reciprocal equation.','250 adds distances rather than solving the lens relation.'],kind='numerical',formula=[LENS],steps=['1/d_i=1/50−1/200=3/200 mm⁻¹.','Invert to obtain d_i=200/3 mm≈66.7 mm.'],locator='1/|zo|+1/|zi|=1/f equation')
ask(c,'For an object moving increasingly far away in the ideal thin-lens real-image model, what happens to the image distance?', ['It approaches the focal length','It approaches zero','It grows in direct proportion to object distance','It approaches twice the focal length for every lens'],['As1/d_o tends to0, the lens equation gives1/d_i→1/f.','Zero distance would not satisfy the limiting reciprocal relation.','The real image approaches the focal plane, not an unbounded distance.','2f occurs for a particular finite object distance, not the infinite-distance limit.'],formula=[LENS],locator='Thin-lens equation',assumptions=['Positive converging lens, real object distance tending to infinity.'])
ask(c,'With d_o=200 mm and d_i=200/3 mm, what image height corresponds to a30 mm upright object under the diagram’s signed vertical convention?', ['−10 mm','+10 mm','−90 mm','+30 mm'],['Signed magnification is−d_i/d_o=−1/3, so the inverted image height is−10 mm.','This has the correct magnitude but misses inversion.','This uses the reciprocal distance ratio.','This assumes unchanged orientation and unit magnification.'],kind='numerical',formula=[LENS],steps=['m=−(200/3)/200=−1/3.','Image height=m×30=−10 mm.'],assumptions=['Object and image heights use the same upward-positive direction; ideal real inverted image.'],locator='Object/image ray diagram and thin-lens relation',citekind='derivation')
ask(c,'Why should the slide’s absolute-distance equation not be extended blindly to a virtual-image problem?', ['Virtual-image calculations require a consistent signed-distance convention not specified by those positive real distances','A virtual image always has a negative light intensity','Every virtual image must lie exactly at the principal point','The focal length has to be converted to pixels before any lens equation can be used'],['The drawn equation is scoped to real object and image distances; signs matter in broader cases.','Image location and intensity sign are different concepts.','The principal point is a camera-image coordinate, not every virtual-image location.','The thin-lens equation works in consistent physical length units.'],locator='Absolute-value thin-lens equation and real-image diagram',assumptions=['Do not introduce a virtual-image sign convention that the source does not define.'])
c=concept(942,'Central perspective projection','Virtual-plane sign, perspective division, depth/scale ambiguity and focal/depth sensitivities.')
PROJ=fm('x=fX/Z; y=fY/Z',{'X,Y,Z':'point coordinates in the camera frame','f':'positive focal length','x,y':'coordinates relative to the principal point on the front virtual image plane'},{'X,Y,Z,f,x,y':'m unless another consistent length unit is explicitly stated'},['Ideal undistorted pinhole; front virtual plane; Z>0 for a visible point; camera x/y directions align with image x/y; no occlusion model.'])
ask(c,'Which projection matches the lecture’s virtual image plane placed in front of the camera center?', ['x=fX/Z, y=fY/Z','x=−fX/Z, y=−fY/Z','x=fZ/X, y=fZ/Y','x=X/f, y=Y/f'],['The front virtual plane convention gives the displayed positive ratios.','Those negative signs correspond to a different rear-plane convention.','This exchanges numerator and denominator and is not the shown similar-triangle relation.','This omits depth and has incompatible physical units for image coordinates.'],formula=[PROJ],locator='x=fX/Z and y=fY/Z equations')
ask(c,'Under ideal perspective projection, which change leaves image coordinates unchanged?', ['Multiplying X,Y,Z by the same positive factor while keeping f fixed','Doubling X and Y while keeping Z and f fixed','Doubling f while keeping X,Y,Z fixed','Halving Z while keeping X,Y,f fixed'],['The common scale cancels from both coordinate ratios.','This doubles the image coordinates.','This doubles their distance from the principal point.','This also doubles the image coordinates.'],formula=[PROJ],locator='Projection equations',assumptions=['Nonzero coordinates where needed; compare coordinates relative to the principal point.'])
ask(c,'Which statement correctly describes the mathematical linearity of perspective projection?', ['The homogeneous matrix map is linear, but division by depth makes ordinary Cartesian projection nonlinear','The Cartesian map is linear in X,Y,Z for all variable depths','Homogeneous coordinates eliminate the need to divide by their third component','The depth division makes each image point determine a unique 3D distance'],['The linear map precedes the nonlinear normalization step.','The1/Z dependence breaks Cartesian linearity as depth varies.','Recovering ordinary coordinates still requires normalization.','Points on the same viewing ray remain ambiguous.'],pages=[942,943],locator='Cartesian equations and homogeneous reformulation')
ask(c,'A point with camera coordinates(X,Y,Z)=(0.2,−0.1,2) m is viewed with f=0.02 m. What is its virtual-plane image coordinate?', ['(0.002,−0.001) m','(0.004,−0.002) m','(0.02,−0.01) m','(−0.002,0.001) m'],['Divide fX and fY by2 to obtain2 mm and−1 mm.','This omits division by depth.','This is ten times too large.','This applies rear-plane sign reversal despite the front-plane convention.'],kind='numerical',formula=[PROJ],steps=['x=0.02×0.2/2=0.002 m.','y=0.02×(−0.1)/2=−0.001 m.'],locator='Projection equations')
ask(c,'What happens to the stated central-projection formula at Z=0 for a nonzero lateral point?', ['The ordinary image coordinate is undefined because normalization divides by zero','The point maps to the principal point regardless of X,Y','The formula automatically assigns zero depth to the output pixel','The point must be rounded to pixel(0,0)'],['The point lies on the camera-center plane and has no finite ordinary projection in this model.','Only a point on the optical axis at nonzero depth maps to the principal point.','A2D image coordinate is not a depth assignment.','Integer rounding does not repair division by zero.'],formula=[PROJ],locator='Denominator Z in both projection equations',assumptions=['This is the boundary of the stated Z>0 model, not an accepted visible point.'])
c=concept(943,'Homogeneous projection and camera pose','Matrix dimensions, dehomogenization, scale invariance and inverse rigid pose.')
POSE=fm('P_C=Rᵀ(P_W−t); T_WC=[[R,t],[0,1]]; T_CW=T_WC⁻¹',{'P_W':'point coordinates in world frame','P_C':'same point coordinates in camera frame','R':'rotation mapping camera vectors into world coordinates','t':'camera origin in world coordinates','T_WC':'homogeneous camera-to-world pose','T_CW':'world-to-camera coordinate transform'},{'P_W,P_C,t':'m','R':'dimensionless','T_WC,T_CW':'homogeneous matrices with dimensionless rotation and metre translation blocks'},['Right-handed rigid frames; RᵀR=I; homogeneous point final coordinate1; projection requires positive resulting camera Z.'])
ask(c,'Which dimensions make the metric homogeneous camera equation valid?', ['A3×4 projection matrix times a4×1 homogeneous point gives a3×1 homogeneous image','A4×3 matrix times a4×1 point gives a3×1 image','A3×3 matrix directly multiplies a4×1 homogeneous point','A4×4 pose matrix alone returns a2×1 pixel coordinate'],['The inner dimensions4 agree and the product has three homogeneous components.','The inner dimensions3 and4 do not agree.','A3×3 matrix cannot directly multiply a4×1 vector.','A pose transform preserves homogeneous3D coordinates; projection is still needed.'],locator='Metric camera matrix C and homogeneous point equation')
ask(c,'The homogeneous image vector is(0.004,−0.002,2)ᵀ. What ordinary image coordinate does it represent?', ['(0.002,−0.001)','(0.004,−0.002)','(0.008,−0.004)','(2,2)'],['Divide the first two components by the third:2.','This omits dehomogenization.','This multiplies by the scale instead of dividing.','The third homogeneous component is not both image coordinates.'],kind='numerical',steps=['x=0.004/2=0.002.','y=−0.002/2=−0.001.'],formula=[fm('x=tilde_x/tilde_z; y=tilde_y/tilde_z',{'tilde_x,tilde_y,tilde_z':'components of the homogeneous image representative','x,y':'ordinary metric image coordinates'},{'x,y':'m in this supplied representative','tilde_x,tilde_y':'m² for the source construction(fX,fY,Z)','tilde_z':'m for that construction'},['Nonzero homogeneous scale; representative is supplied consistently with the source metric projection.'])],locator='Homogeneous image normalization')
ask(c,'If T_WC maps camera-frame coordinates into world coordinates, which operation prepares a world point for the source’s camera-frame projection matrix?', ['Multiply the world homogeneous point by T_WC⁻¹','Multiply it by T_WC again','Apply only the rotation R and ignore the camera origin','Add the camera translation directly to every world point'],['Projection requires camera coordinates, so the camera-to-world pose must be inverted.','This transforms in the opposite coordinate direction.','Translation matters when the camera origin differs from the world origin.','Camera coordinates require subtracting the origin before inverse rotation, not adding it.'],formula=[POSE],locator='World-point equation using inverse camera pose')
ask(c,'A camera has no rotation and world origin t=(−0.5,0,0) m. What camera coordinates represent world point(0.3,0.4,5) m?', ['(0.8,0.4,5) m','(−0.2,0.4,5) m','(0.3,0.4,4.5) m','(0.3,0.9,5) m'],['Subtract camera origin:0.3−(−0.5)=0.8; the other coordinates are unchanged.','This adds the camera translation instead of subtracting it.','The translation is along x, not depth.','The translation does not affect y.'],kind='numerical',formula=[POSE],steps=['With R=I, P_C=P_W−t.','Compute(0.3+0.5,0.4,5)=(0.8,0.4,5) m.'],locator='Inverse-pose transformation',pages=[943,947])
ask(c,'Camera-to-world rotation is R=[[0,−1,0],[1,0,0],[0,0,1]], camera origin t=(1,2,0) m, and world point P_W=(1,3,4) m. What is P_C?', ['(1,0,4) m','(−1,0,4) m','(2,−3,4) m','(5,−2,4) m'],['P_W−t=(0,1,4), then Rᵀ gives(1,0,4).','This uses R instead of Rᵀ after subtraction.','This applies Rᵀ to the point first and then subtracts the unrotated origin.','This uses Rᵀ(P_W+t), adding rather than subtracting the origin.'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[POSE],steps=['Subtract the camera origin to obtain(0,1,4) m.','Rᵀ=[[0,1,0],[−1,0,0],[0,0,1]].','Multiply to obtain(1,0,4) m.'],locator='Inverse-pose transformation',assumptions=['The supplied rotation maps camera axes into world coordinates; use its inverse for world-to-camera conversion.'])
persist()
c=concept(944,'Discrete sensor geometry','Pixel indices, physical pitch, physical sensor dimensions and the offset principal point.')
